<description>
**Problem:** #34931 made unreadable coin entries fatal, but `CDBWrapper::Read()` still returns `false` for deserialization failures at every other caller and propagates LevelDB point-read exceptions back to them.
A corrupt txindex point read can consequently fail one RPC while the node continues running.
Chainstate also retains `CCoinsViewErrorCatcher` as a separate view layer solely to invoke its fatal-error callback.

**Fix:** Make LevelDB point-read and typed deserialization failures fatal in `CDBWrapper`, after logging the database path and error and invoking the fatal-notification callback when one was provided.
Missing keys still return `false`, database-open failures keep using the existing initialization paths, and iterator decoding remains unchanged.
Pass the callback at construction for chainstate, the block-index database, and optional index databases, remove `CCoinsViewErrorCatcher`, keep `HaveCoin()` on the cache view that owns its caching behavior, and mark coin-view lookups `noexcept`.
</description>

<commits>
d584a31694 refactor: reuse LevelDB reads for existence checks

`Exists()` and typed reads both fetch the complete stored value through LevelDB.
Reuse `ReadImpl()` for existence checks so that later changes need one lookup path to update.
Extract key serialization and lookup into `ReadRaw()` for reuse by `Read()`.
Keep `TryRead()` unchanged to preserve its exception handling.

cd5dba88ae init: route database read error notifications

Pass one read-error callback through chainstate, block-index, and optional-index database construction.
Use `KernelNotifications::fatalError()` so these failures report the standard fatal warning and request shutdown before the read path aborts.
Keep the existing error catcher using the same callback until it is removed.

e80ce4c088 test: characterize database point-read errors

When a txindex point read encounters a corrupt LevelDB table, the RPC reports the error and the node continues running.
`CDBWrapper::Read()` throws on a LevelDB failure without running the read-error callback and reports a corrupt value as a missing key.
Record that behavior before making all typed point-read failures fatal.
Keep database-open failures covered separately from point-read failures, and verify that cold-cache coin reads through `gettxout` already abort.

Co-authored-by: Andrew Toth <andrewstoth@gmail.com>

09b9e019f5 dbwrapper: abort on LevelDB read failures

Log LevelDB point-read failures, invoke the read-error callback, and abort so an index read error cannot leave the node running.
Keep the full database path in diagnostics so index databases named `db` can be distinguished.
Missing keys still return the existing not-found result, and database-open failures retain their existing handling.

Co-authored-by: MarcoFalke <*~=`'#}+{/-|&$^_@721217.xyz>

b463984ed6 refactor: extract fatal database read handling

Extract the logging, callback, and abort sequence from the LevelDB read path so deserialization failures can reuse the same policy.

97d1c0bd01 dbwrapper: abort on deserialization failures

Make `Read()` abort on deserialization failures and return `false` only for missing keys.
Keep `TryRead()` and its callers unchanged until the following cleanup.

Co-authored-by: MarcoFalke <*~=`'#}+{/-|&$^_@721217.xyz>

ea983905e1 coins, dbwrapper: remove obsolete read handling

`CDBWrapper` now handles fatal point-read failures directly, and `Read()` returns `false` only for missing keys.
Use `Read()` for coin lookups and remove `TryRead()`, its status types, and their tests.
Direct coin-database reads now abort on deserialization errors instead of throwing, while the node's fatal outcome is unchanged.
Remove the redundant index existence checks and `CCoinsViewErrorCatcher`, connecting the coins cache directly to its database view.
The read-error callback already reaches the database through `DBParams`.

Mark `Read()` `[[nodiscard]]` and make intentional discards explicit.

a6db6e861a coins: make coin view lookups `noexcept`

Mark coin-view lookup methods and cache helpers `noexcept` now that database read failures terminate in `CDBWrapper`.
This records that callers cannot recover from lookup failures.

6c07101f28 coins: limit `HaveCoin` to cache view

`HaveCoin()` is only used on `CCoinsViewCache` in production, where cache misses already fetch through `GetCoin()`.
Remove it from the backing-view interface and direct database view without adding coin copies to cache checks.
Check that `HaveInputs()` fetches cache misses through the backing view's `GetCoin()` and that cached lookups and spends avoid further backing reads.

Co-authored-by: Andrew Toth <andrewstoth@gmail.com>
Co-authored-by: sedited <seb.kung@gmail.com>
Co-authored-by: Novo <eunovo9@gmail.com>
Co-authored-by: Lőrinc <pap.lorinc@gmail.com>
</commits>

